  "evidence": "- TEXT_C: EU membership would continue; open borders with England; full citizenship/residence rights for all current residents. Written constitution and Bill of Rights with social rights; proportional representation and votes at 16; abolish unelected influence and reform the monarchy. Strong environmentalism and phasing out nuclear power; end nuclear reprocessing; withdraw from NATO and remove Trident. Law and order framed with 'zero tolerance' but also alternatives to prison and rehabilitation.\n- TEXT_E: Tough on crime and its causes; halve time to sentencing for young offenders; new offences (e.g., racial harassment); handgun ban. Defence rooted in NATO; retain Trident. EU engagement framed as reformist and subject to tests/referendum on EMU; incorporate ECHR and FOI; devolution by referendum; end hereditary peers' voting rights (not wholesale constitutional rupture). Emphasis on families and welfare-to-work responsibilities.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_C argues chiefly from GAL commitments—continuity of EU membership and open borders, expansive rights and democratic reforms (PR, votes at 16), strong environmentalism and anti‑nuclear stances including exiting NATO—despite some order-focused rhetoric. TEXT_E mixes liberal reforms with clearer TAN-leaning priorities on order and national security (tough on crime, NATO anchoring and Trident retention, pragmatic/conditional EU steps), placing it closer to a slightly TAN electorate.",
